# Global rules (agent-sandbox {{ _agent_sandbox_host_word }})

## Environment

{% if agent_sandbox_host_kind == 'vm' %}
You run in the `{{ inventory_hostname }}` VM ({{ ansible_facts['distribution'] }} {{ ansible_facts['distribution_release'] }}) as user `{{ agent_sandbox_user }}`, without
`sudo`, with rootless podman. Check its resources with `nproc`, `free -h` and `df -h /`
when they matter; they change with the VM definition. You own your home directory and your
containers; packages and system config come from provisioning, so if a task needs a system
change, ask the user. Effects outside the VM are real: git pushes go to the user's real
hosts, and the VM disk is a file on the host's disk.
{% else %}
You run on {{ agent_sandbox_server_description }} as user `{{ agent_sandbox_user }}`, without `sudo`, with
rootless podman. Check its resources with `nproc`, `free -h` and `df -h /` when they
matter; they change with the server specs. You own your home directory and your
containers; packages and system config come from provisioning, so if a task needs a system
change, ask the user. Effects outside it are real: git pushes go to the user's real hosts.

The server is on the public internet and hardened by provisioning: SSH key-only, ufw
denies all incoming traffic except ssh, IPv6 is off. Don't open ports, disable ufw or
change sshd config; if a task needs that, ask the user (ports are managed in their IaC).
Don't expose services on public interfaces; bind to 127.0.0.1.
{% endif %}

Claude Code (`claude`) and opencode (`opencode`) both run as `agent` and share this home,
its repos, the SSH key and `~/.agent/memories`. Another agent may have used the same
checkout: run `git status` before assuming the working tree is yours.

This file is provisioned (as `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` and `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md`)
and overwritten on every provision. To change global rules, write the proposed text to
`~/handoff-vm-manager.md` and tell the user; don't edit this file.

Nothing {{ 'in' if agent_sandbox_host_kind == 'vm' else 'on' }} the {{ _agent_sandbox_host_word }} survives a rebuild except what provisioning installs: push your work,
and expect tool logins and tokens to be gone after a rebuild.

### Git over SSH

Use `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` (the default key) and SSH remotes (`git@host:org/repo.git`).
Never replace or regenerate it. If a host answers `Permission denied (publickey)`, show
the user `~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub` and ask them to add it. For a new host, accept its key
once with `ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new -T git@<host>`.
Ask before force-pushing, deleting remote branches or tags, or pushing to a default branch.

### Disk

{% if agent_sandbox_host_kind == 'vm' %}
The VM's disk lives on the host's disk; filling it suspends the VM.
{% else %}
The server's disk is small and fixed; filling it breaks the server.
{% endif %}
- Run `df -h /` before container image builds or large builds.
- Remove superseded images; after an interrupted build run `podman image prune -f` and
  `rm -rf /var/tmp/buildah*`.
- Keep build output inside the project and prune incremental caches that grow past ~10 GB.
{% if agent_sandbox_host_kind == 'vm' %}
- After deleting a lot, ask the user to run `sudo fstrim -av` so the host gets the space back.
{% endif %}

## Working with the user

- Before building a feature, list its design decisions with a recommendation for each and
  ask about the ones with real trade-offs (data model, on-disk or wire formats, visible UX).
  Decide small mechanical choices yourself and mention them.
- The user's personal data never comes to this {{ _agent_sandbox_host_word }}. To diagnose it, give the user commands
  that print only counts or sums (e.g. jq one-liners).

## Subagents and models

Standing permission (user, 2026-10-12): delegate the work below to subagents without
asking, and name the model tier on every spawn. The main session plans, triages, decides
and talks to the user; it doesn't reproduce, hand-test or read long logs itself. Make a
small edit directly when that is cheaper than briefing an agent.

| Tier     | Claude Code | opencode       | Use for |
|----------|-------------|----------------|---------|
| strong   | Opus        | DeepSeek Pro   | fix batches (find, fix, verify by hand); bug hunting; reviews that decide what protects data; planning QA rounds and writing their report |
| standard | Sonnet      | DeepSeek Flash | hand testing and re-checking fixes; applying an agreed list; docs restructuring; mechanical cleanup |
| light    | Haiku       | DeepSeek Flash | searches and lookups; summarizing long output (tests, linters, CI logs); checking that paths and names in notes still exist. Never code changes or reviews |

No agent: releases; tiny fully specified steps (applying a checked patch, one test run);
waiting on CI with a background loop that prints only the jobs' results. If your tool
can't pick a model per spawn, use the closest one it offers and say so.

Only one agent at a time changes a git working tree; read-only agents may run in parallel.

### Delegating from Claude Code to opencode

Claude Code's own agent tools can't reach opencode, but Claude Code can hand work to the
DeepSeek tiers by running opencode headless from its shell. DeepSeek costs the user API
money, so Claude Code's default stays its own tiers (Opus, Sonnet, Haiku). Use opencode
only for hand testing (emulator, TUI, QA rounds), when Claude's session limit is running
low, or when the user asks for it.

1. Write a brief to `~/handoff-<task>.md`: the tier and model, the rules (read-only, or
   which files it may change), steps that name exact commands, paths and expected results,
   where to write the report (`~/handoff-<task>-report.md`), and "if blocked, write why in
   the report and stop". The run can't ask questions.
2. Run it in the background with the message **before** `-f`. `-f` takes a list, so a
   message after it is read as another file and the run fails with "File not found":
   `opencode run "Follow the attached hand-off note exactly." -m deepseek/deepseek-flash --dir <repo> --auto --title "<task>" -f ~/handoff-<task>.md > <scratchpad>/opencode-<task>.log 2>&1; echo "exit $?"; git -C <repo> status --short`
   Models: `deepseek/deepseek-flash` (standard/light), `deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro` (strong);
   `opencode models` lists them. `--auto` approves permission prompts, since nobody is
   there to answer them.
3. Check that it started: the log begins with `> build · deepseek-...` and
   `opencode session list` shows the title. If `opencode auth list` doesn't show DeepSeek,
   the login is gone (e.g. after a rebuild): ask the user to run `opencode auth login`.
4. When it exits, read the exit code, `git status` (a read-only brief leaves no new
   changes) and the report. Don't read the log in the main session. Check every finding
   against the code before acting on it.
5. A report can't prove "found" claims: spot-check a few yourself, or have a light agent
   audit `opencode export <session id>` (the full session as JSON, every tool call).
6. Follow up in the same session with `opencode run "<question>" -s <session id> -m ...`.
   The user can open it in opencode.

The one-agent-per-working-tree rule covers these runs too: while a run that writes is
going, the main session doesn't edit that checkout.

### QA rounds

1. Strong plans: reads the project notes and backlog, writes one prompt per interface the
   project has (e.g. CLI/TUI, app, web).
2. Standard, round 1: one agent per interface, in parallel, reporting only.
3. Strong finds the gaps: what each agent missed, judged by what the others tried.
4. Standard, round 2: checks those gaps.
5. Strong writes the final report: drops what is decided or known, sorts findings into bug
   with repro / UX suggestion / decision for the user, checks every high-priority finding
   against the code.
6. The user decides; then strong agents fix in batches.

## Tests

Add a test when a bug could lose, corrupt or split data (storage, sync, merge, undo,
import/export, on-disk formats, money/number parsing), or when a behaviour has no other
test. Test logic once, at the lowest layer that owns it; UI tests check only that keys and
calls are wired. Cosmetic fixes get no new test; check them by hand.

## Project notes

Keep a project's CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md short: how to work on that project. Design goes in
its design doc, open items in a backlog file, release history in release commits. A
folder's own notes go in that folder's CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md. (opencode reads a project
CLAUDE.md only when there is no AGENTS.md.)

## Memory

Shared notes for both agents live in `~/.agent/memories/<topic>/<subtopic>.md`. Use only
this store, not any built-in memory feature.

- Find them with `find ~/.agent/memories -name '*.md'`; `README.md` there has one-line
  descriptions. When a task touches a topic, read only the files whose path matches it.
  Do this again when the topic changes.
- Memories may be out of date. The user's current instructions and what you observe on the
  machine win; fix or remove a memory you find to be wrong.
- When the user says "remember …", "don't forget …" or similar, append one line to the
  best-fitting file, creating it if needed, and tell the user which file you used:
  `printf -- '- %s: %s\n' "$(date +%F)" '<text>' >> ~/.agent/memories/<topic>/<subtopic>.md`
  Append with the shell; don't rewrite the file (the other agent may be writing to it).
- When you create a file, add a line for it under its topic heading in `README.md`.
- `/remember <text>` (a custom command in both agents) does the same as "remember that <text>".
